# Contributing to <App Name>

`new-test-app` copies this to the fork root and fills `<App Name>` / `<slug>`. It is the
one-page onboarding doc for a developer working on this application.

## What is app-owned vs framework-owned

This repo is a **fork of the Super_Test_App framework** (`docs/TEMPLATE.md` §1 is the
authoritative boundary). You own:

- `app/<slug>/` — step-type packages, variable maps, recipes, `controller.json`, `VERSION`
- `backend/modules/<slug>_*/` — app-specific modules
- `instrument_libs/` — drivers copied from the central `Instrument_Library`
- `frontend/src/app/overrides/` — screen overrides
- `labview/App/` and the live config (`backend/config/*.json`)
- **this repo's own** `.github/workflows/release.yml` and `deploy/cut-release.ps1`

Everything else is framework code. A change you need there goes upstream and comes back as a
framework release you `git merge` — never a downstream patch (it makes the next merge worse).

## Local setup

```pwsh
# backend
cd backend
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"        # or ".[dev,release]" to also cut releases (below)
pytest -q
cd ..
# frontend
cd frontend
npm install
npx vitest run
npm run build                            # this is the type check
cd ..
# run the whole stack
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\dev.ps1
```

Dev login `admin` / `admin`. Live config is gitignored — copy from the `*.example.json` on
first run.

## Cutting a release

Two supported paths — pick one and keep `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s trigger matching it:

| Path | Trigger | Cost | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| **GitHub-hosted** (`release.yml`) | `on: push: tags: ["app-v*"]` | ~45 min / ~90 Free minutes **per release** (Windows bills 2×), and the Nuitka cache never warms — see the note atop `release.yml` | paid plan, or no local Windows toolchain |
| **Local** (`deploy/cut-release.ps1`) | `on: workflow_dispatch:` (retarget `release.yml` so the tag push doesn't fire both) | one warm local Nuitka cache; no CI minutes | GitHub Free, cost-conscious |

Both produce the same GitHub Release with the same four assets
(`<slug>-<ver>.ksupdate`, `<slug>-<ver>.zip`, `run_station.exe`, `<AppShort>-Setup-<ver>.exe`),
so the in-app updater and first-install docs are identical.

`deploy/cut-release.ps1` is the **single build/release entry** — it runs every step
(`fetch-mosquitto.ps1`, `build_release.py`, the signer, `build-installer.ps1`) itself; you never
call those directly. It auto-detects the app under `app/` (pass `-Slug` to override). Use
**`.\deploy\cut-release.ps1 -BuildOnly`** to build the artifacts locally without committing,
tagging, or publishing (no `gh` needed).

### Prerequisites for the local script

- **Python 3.11 or 3.12** + `pip install -e "backend[dev,release]"` (pulls Nuitka, zstandard, pywebview)
- **Node / npm** (frontend build)
- **Inno Setup 6** — `winget install JRSoftware.InnoSetup` (for `setup.exe`)
- **`gh` CLI** authenticated with `contents:write` on `origin`
- A C toolchain Nuitka can use — **MSVC Build Tools** ("Desktop development with C++") is the
  tested one; without it `run_station.exe` / `setup.exe` may fail to build (the script is
  fail-soft and still publishes the two update assets)
- *(optional, trusted signing)* `KS_INTERMEDIATE_SEED` / `KS_INTERMEDIATE_CERT` in the
  environment — without them the `.ksupdate` is dev-signed and installs only where
  `updates.allow_unverified: true`

### Steps

```pwsh
# 1. bump app/<slug>/VERSION (independent semver) and add a CHANGELOG.md section
# 2. run it (slug is baked in as the default):
.\deploy\cut-release.ps1
#    -DryRun        stop before any push / tag / gh release
#    -SkipTests     resume after a mid-pipeline failure (tag already pushed)
#    -CacheDir <p>  override the persistent Nuitka cache (default %LOCALAPPDATA%\tmf-nuitka-cache)
```

The script guards that `VERSION` is strictly newer than the latest published `app-v*` tag,
runs the tests, then **pushes the `app-v<version>` tag before the build** so a second
developer racing the same version is rejected by git in seconds, not after a 45-minute build.

See `docs/DEPLOY_STATION.md` §2 for the full release + client-update picture.
